UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 31, Lesson 2: Balancing in different ways', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"We can balance on different body parts, at different levels and in different shapes, such as wide, narrow, curled or stretched. Changing the base of support, the shape and the level makes a balance easier or harder. The aim is to hold a balance still for 3 to 5 seconds and then move out of it smoothly. Partners can make matching or contrasting balances while standing apart, without leaning on each other.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhich balance is easier to hold: standing on both feet, or standing on tiptoe on one foot? Give a reason using the words ''base of support''.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStanding on both feet is easier because the base of support is bigger. One foot on tiptoe has a very small base, so tiny movements make you wobble.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving a balance is only good if it is hard. A good balance is still, controlled and safe at a level that suits you.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCreate six different balances using different body parts, levels and shapes, and draw each with a label. Hold each for 5 seconds, order them from easiest to hardest and say why using ''base of support''.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"We can balance on different body parts, at different levels and in different shapes, such as wide, narrow, curled or stretched. Changing the base of support, the shape and the level makes a balance easier or harder. The aim is to hold a balance still for 3 to 5 seconds and then move out of it smoothly. Partners can make matching or contrasting balances while standing apart, without leaning on each other.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-MSP-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Create six different balances using different body parts, levels and shapes, and draw each with a label. Hold each for 5 seconds, order them from easiest to hardest and say why using ''base of support''. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using body parts on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Believing a balance is only good if it is hard.. Correction: Standing on both feet is easier because the base of support is bigger.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing a balance is only good if it is hard. A good balance is still, controlled and safe at a level that suits you. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating body parts on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with body parts, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about body parts with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for body parts. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for body parts. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Standing on both feet is easier because the base of support is bigger","Believing a balance is only good if it is hard","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson"],"question":"Which balance is easier to hold: standing on both feet, or standing on tiptoe on one foot? Give a reason using the words ''base of support''. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for body parts; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing a balance is only good if it is hard","We can balance on different body parts, at different levels and in different shapes, such as wide, narrow, curled or","Standing on both feet is easier because the base of support is bigger","Careful use of level and shape and explain why it matte with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with body parts?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named level and shape and explain why it matte, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply body parts to new material and name the level and shape and explain why it matte used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Believing a balance is only good if it is hard","Name level and shape and explain why it matte only, without linking it to body parts"],"question":"Which response best applies body parts with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on body parts. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing a balance is only good if it is hard","Standing on both feet is easier because the base of support is bigger","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for body parts?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Believing a balance is only good if it is hard","Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with body parts"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about body parts?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (We can balance on different body parts, at different levels and in) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a put-down and call it honesty","Example: Kim makes a ''low and wide'' balance (on hands and feet), then changes to a ''high and narrow'' balance (tiptoe","Believing a balance is only good if it is hard","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about body parts?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for body parts?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing a balance is only good if it is hard","Name level and shape and explain why it matte only, with no linked example","Create six different balances using different body parts, levels and shapes, and draw each with a label","Retype the worked example with no new example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies body parts to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about body parts with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating body parts on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using body parts on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for body parts. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with body parts, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Believing a balance is only good if it is hard.. Correction: Standing on both feet is easier because the base of support is bigger.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w31-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise body parts.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Movement Skill and Performance","success_criteria":["I can use body parts with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify level and shape and explain why it matters.","I can practise a still hold independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a smooth exit needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"We can balance on different body parts, at different levels and in different shapes, such as wide, narrow, curled or stretched.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","We can balance on different body parts, at different levels and in different shapes, such as wide, narrow, curled or","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about body parts with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Kim makes a ''low and wide'' balance (on hands and feet), then changes to a ''high and narrow'' balance (tiptoe, arms up). She holds each for 5 seconds. The narrow balance wobbles more because the base is smaller. Partner task: one partner makes a wide balance and the other makes a narrow one.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhich balance is easier to hold: standing on both feet, or standing on tiptoe on one foot? Give a reason using the words ''base of support''.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStanding on both feet is easier because the base of support is bigger. One foot on tiptoe has a very small base, so tiny movements make you wobble.","explain":"We can balance on different body parts, at different levels and in different shapes, such as wide, narrow, curled or stretched. Changing the base of support, the shape and the level makes a balance easier or harder. The aim is to hold a balance still for 3 to 5 seconds and then move out of it smoothly. Partners can make matching or contrasting balances while standing apart, without leaning on each other.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing a balance is only good if it is hard. A good balance is still, controlled and safe at a level that suits you. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Low and high balances.","caption":"Change level and shape.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Low and wide</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">hands and feet</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">High and narrow</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">tiptoe, arms up</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or